<b>ו.</b> "And at the end of the night" - this is the time of "shomrim la-boker," even in summer. If near sunrise, first say kinot and then begin "Shomrim la-boker" (Derekh Chokhmah). Regarding tikkun chatzot, see writings of kavanot and Zohar Vayakhel: night is always reckoned as twelve hours in summer and winter, though prayer times differ (siman 233, Magen Avraham). Some later authorities accepted the mnemonic "from shichvi to kumi" (from Shavuot to Tisha b'Av one need not rise at night). Others say from 15 Av to 15 Iyar one should rise. Others give eight hours as sleep measure; all depends on the individual. Ari z"l said Mishnah study comes before all else then; through it one merits neshamah (Mishnah/neshamah), and if one merits hidden wisdom, that time is especially fit.
